---
title:          "Public preferences for democracy aid in post-conflict countries" 
date:           2026-08-01
category:       "Work in Progress"
abstract: >-
          Democracy aid is increasingly given to countries with a history of war and political violence, however with limited success. To increase the aid’s efficacy in supporting democratization, democracy assistance must align with local needs and preferences, yet we lack knowledge about which types of foreign and democracy aid citizens in these settings prefer. This project investigates recipient public attitudes towards democracy aid in four post-conflict countries: Armenia, Georgia, Serbia, and Ukraine. Using conjoint survey experiments with 6,000 respondents, we will identify which donors, aid types and delivery channels publics in post-conflict states prefer. The findings will offer guidance on how to design democracy aid in a way that aligns with local preferences and supports the aid’s efficacy in promoting democratization in post-conflict states.


authors:
  - Leonie Reicheneder
  - Magnus Lundgren
